1
00:00:00,119 --> 00:00:02,047
Two classes take the same quiz.

2
00:00:02,486 --> 00:00:05,199
Both classes have an average score of five out of ten.

3
00:00:05,625 --> 00:00:07,127
Are the two classes the same?

4
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:08,085
Not at all.

5
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:11,555
In one class, almost everybody scored five.

6
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:15,851
In the other class, some students scored two and some scored eight.

7
00:00:16,276 --> 00:00:18,244
The average hides this difference.

8
00:00:18,683 --> 00:00:20,917
We need a second number that measures spread.

9
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:24,926
Machine learning needs that number all the time.

10
00:00:25,351 --> 00:00:29,673
We often rescale every input so that its spread is one.

11
00:00:30,099 --> 00:00:36,442
Inside every GPT block, layer normalization computes a spread for each token.

12
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:42,373
In this video you will compute variance, standard deviation and covariance by hand.

13
00:00:42,812 --> 00:00:45,325
You need the expected value from video thirteen.

14
00:00:47,052 --> 00:00:49,619
Here are the two classes as dot plots.

15
00:00:50,031 --> 00:00:52,824
Each dot is one student, placed at its score.

16
00:00:53,249 --> 00:00:56,082
This is a toy example with five students per class.

17
00:00:56,521 --> 00:01:00,683
Class A has the scores four, five, five, five and six.

18
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:04,965
Class B has two, four, five, six and eight.

19
00:01:05,404 --> 00:01:09,021
Both lists add up to twenty-five, so both means are five.

20
00:01:09,939 --> 00:01:10,684
Remember the mean.

21
00:01:11,109 --> 00:01:15,923
It is the expected value: each value times its probability, added up.

22
00:01:16,349 --> 00:01:21,495
With five equally likely students, every probability is one fifth.

23
00:01:21,921 --> 00:01:24,341
So the mean is the balance point of the dots.

24
00:01:24,806 --> 00:01:27,785
For both classes, the balance point sits at five.

25
00:01:28,224 --> 00:01:29,607
The center is the same.

26
00:01:30,006 --> 00:01:31,003
The spread is not.

27
00:01:31,939 --> 00:01:33,069
How can we measure spread?

28
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:37,498
A natural idea: measure how far each dot is from the mean.

29
00:01:37,937 --> 00:01:41,447
That distance, with its sign, is called a deviation.

30
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:49,359
For class B, the deviations are minus three, minus one, zero, plus one and plus three.

31
00:01:49,772 --> 00:01:52,298
Dots on the left get negative deviations.

32
00:01:52,710 --> 00:01:55,117
Dots on the right get positive ones.

33
00:01:56,073 --> 00:01:57,894
Now average the deviations.

34
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:04,038
Minus three, minus one, zero, one and three add up to zero.

35
00:02:04,477 --> 00:02:06,817
So the average deviation is zero.

36
00:02:07,256 --> 00:02:08,506
And this is not bad luck.

37
00:02:08,958 --> 00:02:13,453
The mean is the balance point, so the deviations always cancel.

38
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:17,190
The plain average of deviations is always zero.

39
00:02:17,615 --> 00:02:19,237
It tells us nothing about spread.

40
00:02:20,973 --> 00:02:24,457
The fix is to square each deviation before averaging.

41
00:02:24,882 --> 00:02:28,113
A square is never negative, so nothing can cancel.

42
00:02:28,552 --> 00:02:30,387
Minus three squared is nine.

43
00:02:30,879 --> 00:02:32,688
Three squared is also nine.

44
00:02:33,113 --> 00:02:37,223
The left side and the right side now both add to the total.

45
00:02:37,648 --> 00:02:40,959
Big deviations count a lot, small ones count a little.

46
00:02:41,873 --> 00:02:43,136
This gives the definition.

47
00:02:43,561 --> 00:02:48,920
The variance of X is the expected value of X minus mu, all squared.

48
00:02:49,333 --> 00:02:50,370
Read it from the inside.

49
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:52,232
X is the random value.

50
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:53,362
Mu is its mean.

51
00:02:53,774 --> 00:02:55,928
X minus mu is the deviation.

52
00:02:56,367 --> 00:02:57,072
We square it.

53
00:02:57,498 --> 00:03:02,697
Then E, the expected value, averages the squares with their probabilities.

54
00:03:03,639 --> 00:03:05,248
Let's compute it for class B.

55
00:03:05,687 --> 00:03:11,392
The deviations are minus three, minus one, zero, one and three.

56
00:03:11,844 --> 00:03:15,195
The squares are nine, one, zero, one and nine.

57
00:03:15,634 --> 00:03:16,631
They add up to twenty.

58
00:03:17,057 --> 00:03:21,246
Each student has probability one fifth, so we divide by five.

59
00:03:21,671 --> 00:03:24,158
Twenty divided by five is four.

60
00:03:24,597 --> 00:03:26,817
The variance of class B is four.

61
00:03:27,773 --> 00:03:28,890
Now class A.

62
00:03:29,315 --> 00:03:34,767
The deviations are minus one, zero, zero, zero and one.

63
00:03:35,206 --> 00:03:39,288
The squares are one, zero, zero, zero and one.

64
00:03:39,714 --> 00:03:40,512
They add up to two.

65
00:03:40,924 --> 00:03:43,757
Two divided by five is zero point four.

66
00:03:44,195 --> 00:03:48,743
So class A has variance zero point four, and class B has variance four.

67
00:03:49,169 --> 00:03:51,549
Ten times more spread, in one number.

68
00:03:53,106 --> 00:03:54,077
Two facts follow.

69
00:03:54,502 --> 00:03:58,186
A variance is never negative, because it averages squares.

70
00:03:58,624 --> 00:04:01,909
It is zero only when every value equals the mean.

71
00:04:02,335 --> 00:04:06,962
And a common mistake is to forget the square: then you always get zero.

72
00:04:08,706 --> 00:04:11,552
There is one problem with variance: its units.

73
00:04:11,977 --> 00:04:13,121
The scores are in points.

74
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:18,506
But we squared the deviations, so the variance of four is in points squared.

75
00:04:18,919 --> 00:04:21,113
A points squared is hard to imagine.

76
00:04:21,525 --> 00:04:25,182
To go back to the original units, we take the square root.

77
00:04:26,106 --> 00:04:29,510
The square root of the variance is called the standard deviation.

78
00:04:29,949 --> 00:04:31,385
Its symbol is sigma.

79
00:04:31,851 --> 00:04:35,281
For class B, the square root of four is two.

80
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:38,380
So the standard deviation is two points.

81
00:04:38,805 --> 00:04:41,332
You can read it as a typical distance from the mean.

82
00:04:41,797 --> 00:04:47,130
For class A, the square root of zero point four is about zero point six three.

83
00:04:48,639 --> 00:04:50,900
There is also a shortcut for variance.

84
00:04:51,325 --> 00:04:56,339
The variance equals the expected value of X squared, minus mu squared.

85
00:04:56,764 --> 00:05:00,900
In words: the mean of the squares, minus the square of the mean.

86
00:05:01,339 --> 00:05:04,051
Let's check it on class B, with the same numbers.

87
00:05:04,973 --> 00:05:11,475
The squares of the scores are four, sixteen, twenty-five, thirty-six and sixty-four.

88
00:05:11,901 --> 00:05:14,095
They add up to one hundred forty-five.

89
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:17,034
Divided by five, that is twenty-nine.

90
00:05:17,459 --> 00:05:20,903
The mean is five, and five squared is twenty-five.

91
00:05:21,329 --> 00:05:23,869
Twenty-nine minus twenty-five is four.

92
00:05:24,308 --> 00:05:25,837
The same variance as before.

93
00:05:27,573 --> 00:05:34,634
Now take two variables at the same time, for example hours studied, called x, and quiz score, called y.

94
00:05:35,033 --> 00:05:37,599
Each pair becomes one dot in a scatter plot.

95
00:05:38,025 --> 00:05:39,660
Look at three toy clouds.

96
00:05:40,086 --> 00:05:42,759
In the first, y goes up when x goes up.

97
00:05:43,198 --> 00:05:44,913
In the second, y goes down.

98
00:05:45,325 --> 00:05:46,974
In the third, there is no pattern.

99
00:05:48,459 --> 00:05:50,600
We want one number that tells these three apart.

100
00:05:51,039 --> 00:05:54,656
The idea is to multiply the two deviations.

101
00:05:55,082 --> 00:06:02,954
If a dot is right of the mean of x and above the mean of y, both deviations are positive, and the product is positive.

102
00:06:03,379 --> 00:06:08,127
Left and below gives minus times minus, which is also positive.

103
00:06:08,552 --> 00:06:11,199
The other two corners give negative products.

104
00:06:12,093 --> 00:06:15,085
Average these products, and you get the covariance.

105
00:06:15,523 --> 00:06:20,311
It is the expected value of X minus its mean, times Y minus its mean.

106
00:06:20,736 --> 00:06:24,353
A positive covariance means the variables move together.

107
00:06:24,765 --> 00:06:28,529
A negative covariance means they move in opposite directions.

108
00:06:28,968 --> 00:06:32,678
A covariance near zero means no straight-line pattern.

109
00:06:33,117 --> 00:06:39,539
Our three clouds give zero point five nine, minus zero point six five, and zero point one.

110
00:06:41,073 --> 00:06:42,469
Let's compute one by hand.

111
00:06:42,934 --> 00:06:47,402
Four students studied one, three, five and seven hours.

112
00:06:47,841 --> 00:06:51,152
Their scores were two, three, six and five.

113
00:06:51,578 --> 00:06:52,788
The mean of x is four.

114
00:06:53,227 --> 00:06:54,995
The mean of y is also four.

115
00:06:55,421 --> 00:07:00,474
So the x deviations are minus three, minus one, one and three.

116
00:07:00,913 --> 00:07:05,900
The y deviations are minus two, minus one, two and one.

117
00:07:06,826 --> 00:07:08,169
Multiply the pairs.

118
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:11,467
Minus three times minus two is six.

119
00:07:11,892 --> 00:07:14,499
Minus one times minus one is one.

120
00:07:14,924 --> 00:07:16,493
One times two is two.

121
00:07:16,999 --> 00:07:18,501
Three times one is three.

122
00:07:18,954 --> 00:07:20,815
The products add up to twelve.

123
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:25,044
Divide by four students, and the covariance is three.

124
00:07:25,483 --> 00:07:29,938
It is positive, so more hours go with higher scores in this toy data.

125
00:07:31,473 --> 00:07:32,111
One warning.

126
00:07:32,536 --> 00:07:35,887
Covariance only sees straight-line patterns.

127
00:07:36,326 --> 00:07:40,582
Take x from minus two up to two, and y equal to x squared.

128
00:07:40,994 --> 00:07:45,688
The covariance is zero, even though y depends completely on x.

129
00:07:46,167 --> 00:07:48,946
Zero covariance does not mean unrelated.

130
00:07:50,673 --> 00:07:57,720
With two variables we have three numbers: the variance of x, the variance of y, and their covariance.

131
00:07:58,119 --> 00:08:02,521
We can store them in a two by two matrix, called the covariance matrix.

132
00:08:02,946 --> 00:08:04,821
The variances go on the diagonal.

133
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:08,425
The covariance goes in both off-diagonal places.

134
00:08:09,379 --> 00:08:15,829
For our four students, the variance of x is five, and the variance of y is two point five.

135
00:08:16,281 --> 00:08:18,009
The covariance is three.

136
00:08:18,448 --> 00:08:22,664
So the matrix is five, three, three, two point five.

137
00:08:23,089 --> 00:08:29,924
The matrix is symmetric, because the covariance of x and y equals the covariance of y and x.

138
00:08:30,323 --> 00:08:33,781
With ten features, the matrix would be ten by ten.

139
00:08:35,306 --> 00:08:36,396
Where does all this come from?

140
00:08:36,822 --> 00:08:44,894
The mean is a weighted sum, from axiom A, linearity, with weights that follow axiom D, the probability rules.

141
00:08:45,332 --> 00:08:50,452
Now put the x deviations in one vector and the y deviations in another.

142
00:08:50,891 --> 00:08:56,037
Their dot product is six plus one plus two plus three, which is twelve.

143
00:08:56,463 --> 00:08:58,883
Divide by four, and you get three.

144
00:08:59,806 --> 00:09:03,795
So covariance is an average dot product of deviation vectors.

145
00:09:04,234 --> 00:09:06,654
That is axiom B, the inner product.

146
00:09:07,067 --> 00:09:12,545
Variance is the same thing with a vector and itself: an average squared length.

147
00:09:12,957 --> 00:09:18,104
So variance and covariance are built from axioms A, B and D.

148
00:09:19,639 --> 00:09:20,517
One honest detail.

149
00:09:20,956 --> 00:09:23,682
We divided by n, the number of data points.

150
00:09:24,147 --> 00:09:30,012
For a sample from a larger population, many tools divide by n minus one instead.

151
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:32,006
This is called Bessel's correction.

152
00:09:32,445 --> 00:09:37,924
NumPy's var divides by n by default, but NumPy's cov divides by n minus one.

153
00:09:39,673 --> 00:09:41,707
Now let's use variance in machine learning.

154
00:09:42,133 --> 00:09:44,340
The first use is standardization.

155
00:09:44,806 --> 00:09:49,327
For each value, subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation.

156
00:09:49,752 --> 00:09:51,441
The result is called a z-score.

157
00:09:51,920 --> 00:10:03,476
For class B, the scores two, four, five, six and eight become minus one point five, minus zero point five, zero, zero point five and one point five.

158
00:10:04,406 --> 00:10:09,060
After standardization, the mean is zero and the variance is one.

159
00:10:09,526 --> 00:10:17,478
Features in meters, dollars or years now share one scale, and many learning methods train more stably.

160
00:10:18,993 --> 00:10:24,192
The second use is layer normalization, inside every GPT block.

161
00:10:24,671 --> 00:10:32,078
For one token, the model takes its list of features, computes their mean and variance, and standardizes them.

162
00:10:32,503 --> 00:10:37,224
GPT-2 small has seven hundred sixty-eight features per token.

163
00:10:37,649 --> 00:10:43,886
A tiny number, epsilon, is added to the variance, so the model never divides by zero.

164
00:10:45,439 --> 00:10:50,027
The third use is principal component analysis, or PCA.

165
00:10:50,466 --> 00:10:54,322
It looks for the directions in which the data spreads the most.

166
00:10:54,748 --> 00:10:57,713
Those directions come from the covariance matrix.

167
00:10:58,112 --> 00:11:04,083
Layer normalization and PCA are molecules: they combine this atom with others.

168
00:11:04,522 --> 00:11:06,623
We will meet them in the molecules map.

169
00:11:08,339 --> 00:11:09,204
Now it is your turn.

170
00:11:09,642 --> 00:11:11,517
Pause the video and try this.

171
00:11:11,930 --> 00:11:15,520
The data set is one, one, five and five.

172
00:11:15,932 --> 00:11:18,924
What is its variance, and what is its standard deviation?

173
00:11:19,337 --> 00:11:23,419
Start with the mean, then the deviations, then the squares.

174
00:11:24,366 --> 00:11:25,603
Here is the answer.

175
00:11:26,028 --> 00:11:27,012
The mean is three.

176
00:11:27,438 --> 00:11:32,025
The deviations are minus two, minus two, two and two.

177
00:11:32,438 --> 00:11:34,193
Every square is four.

178
00:11:34,672 --> 00:11:37,810
Four plus four plus four plus four is sixteen.

179
00:11:38,235 --> 00:11:41,081
Divide by four, and the variance is four.

180
00:11:41,507 --> 00:11:45,762
The square root of four is two, so the standard deviation is two.

181
00:11:47,293 --> 00:11:48,051
One more question.

182
00:11:48,516 --> 00:11:52,519
Three points: one, six, then two, four, then three, two.

183
00:11:52,944 --> 00:11:56,787
Is the covariance of x and y positive or negative?

184
00:11:57,213 --> 00:11:57,971
Can you compute it?

185
00:11:58,410 --> 00:11:59,128
Pause and try.

186
00:12:00,039 --> 00:12:03,643
The mean of x is two, and the mean of y is four.

187
00:12:04,082 --> 00:12:10,784
The deviation pairs are minus one and two, then zero and zero, then one and minus two.

188
00:12:11,236 --> 00:12:14,654
The products are minus two, zero and minus two.

189
00:12:15,066 --> 00:12:16,475
They add up to minus four.

190
00:12:16,901 --> 00:12:21,050
Divide by three, and the covariance is minus four thirds.

191
00:12:21,475 --> 00:12:24,773
It is negative: when x goes up, y goes down.

192
00:12:26,506 --> 00:12:29,139
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy.

193
00:12:29,578 --> 00:12:34,870
We make the two classes, compute the deviations, and average their squares.

194
00:12:35,309 --> 00:12:36,253
We check the shortcut.

195
00:12:36,692 --> 00:12:43,221
Then we build the covariance matrix of hours and scores, once with n and once with n minus one.

196
00:12:43,727 --> 00:12:45,735
Finally, we standardize class B.

197
00:12:47,273 --> 00:12:51,235
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

198
00:12:51,661 --> 00:12:55,810
Both means are five, and the deviations average to zero.

199
00:12:56,235 --> 00:12:59,134
The variances are zero point four and four.

200
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:03,536
The shortcut also gives four, and the covariance is three.

201
00:13:03,975 --> 00:13:06,900
With n minus one, the matrix grows.

202
00:13:07,326 --> 00:13:09,081
The z-scores have variance one.

203
00:13:10,839 --> 00:13:11,504
In summary.

204
00:13:11,943 --> 00:13:15,001
Variance is the expected squared distance from the mean.

205
00:13:15,427 --> 00:13:20,174
We square the deviations because plain deviations always cancel to zero.

206
00:13:20,573 --> 00:13:26,743
The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, and it is in the original units.

207
00:13:27,182 --> 00:13:31,650
The shortcut says: the mean of the squares minus the square of the mean.

208
00:13:32,573 --> 00:13:35,977
Covariance is the average product of deviations.

209
00:13:36,416 --> 00:13:41,482
Its sign says whether two variables move together or in opposite directions.

210
00:13:41,908 --> 00:13:46,442
The covariance matrix collects all variances and covariances.

211
00:13:46,854 --> 00:13:53,503
Next, in video fifteen, we meet the loss function: one number that says how wrong a model is.

212
00:13:53,942 --> 00:13:54,514
See you there.

213
00:13:56,439 --> 00:13:58,261
